<Project>

  <!--
    Warning policy for the vendored libraries under libs/.

    Every project here lives in a git submodule pointing at a separate repository, so its source is
    not ours to edit: a fix would need a commit in that repository and a submodule bump in this one,
    and this repo deliberately gates submodule bumps (scripts/security/check-submodule-bump.sh).

    Three warnings come out of Aura.UI, and all three are decisions that library made rather than
    defects we can act on. Left un-suppressed they are the *only* warnings in a clean build of the
    solution, which is precisely how a real warning in our own code goes unnoticed — so they are
    silenced here, at the consumer boundary, and enumerated rather than blanket-disabled.

      CS0108   AuraTabItem.Icon / .IconProperty hide TabItem's members. Intentional in that control:
               it re-declares Icon with its own type. `new` would silence it, in their repository.
      AVA2002  Avalonia's analyzer objecting to a Bitmap constructed from an avares: URI in
               NavigationViewItemBase. Also that library's call, and it works.

    Scoped to these codes on purpose. A blanket NoWarn here would hide the next real problem a
    submodule bump brings in, which is the opposite of the point.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0108;AVA2002</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
